              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The temperature at which food can be accepted during a delivery depends on the type of food. Under standard food safety guidelines (such as the FDA Food Code and ServSafe), the receiving temperatures are broken down as follows:\n\n### 1. Cold TCS Foods (Time/Temperature Control for Safety)\nMost refrigerated foods must be received at **41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower**. \n* *Examples:* Fresh meat, poultry, seafood, cut melons, cut tomatoes, leafy greens, and pre-prepped foods.\n\n### 2. Hot TCS Foods\nHot-prepared foods (such as those delivered for catering or hot-holding) must be received at **135\u00b0F (57\u00b0C) or higher**.\n\n### 3. Frozen Foods\nFrozen foods must be received **frozen solid**. \n* **Reject the delivery if:** There are signs of thawing or temperature abuse, such as fluids, water stains, or large ice crystals on the food or packaging (which indicates the food has thawed and been refrozen).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Specific Exceptions (45\u00b0F / 7\u00b0C)\nThere are a few specific food items that can legally be accepted at **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C)**, provided they meet certain conditions:\n\n| Food Item | Receiving Criteria | Post-Receiving Rule |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Milk** | Received at **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower** | Must be cooled to **41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower** within 4 hours. |\n| **Shell Eggs** | Ambient/air temperature of **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower** | Store immediately in a cooler at 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower. |\n| **Shucked Shellfish** | Received at **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower** | Must be cooled to **41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower** within 4 hours. |\n| **Live Shellfish** (Oysters, clams, mussels, scallops) | Air temp of **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C)** AND internal temp no greater than **50\u00b0F (10\u00b0C)** | Must be cooled to **41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower** within 4 hours. |\n\n---\n\n### Other Crucial Quality Checks Upon Delivery\nEven if the temperature is correct, you should reject food if:\n* **Packaging is damaged:** Look out for tears, leaks, rust, severe dents in cans, or bloated/vacuum-sealed packages.\n* **Signs of spoilage:** Reject food that has an abnormal color, mold, an off-odor, or a slimy/sticky texture.\n* **Missing documentation:** Shellfish must have shellfish tags (which must be kept on file for 90 days after the last shellfish is served), and sushi-grade or farm-raised fish must have proper FDA parasite-destruction documentation.",
